# Two materials exercising the MTL properties added in #1575.
#
# `chrome` is the specular case: a dark base so the highlight is the only
# bright thing on it, a near-white `Ks` and a tight `Ns`. Note `Ks` alone
# does nothing — `Ns` of 0 is the format's "no highlight", which is why a
# matte material can safely carry a bright specular colour.
newmtl chrome
Kd 0.10 0.11 0.14
Ks 1.0 0.97 0.90
Ns 180
# Hammered-and-pitted normal map (#1574). The interesting half is what it does
# to the SPECULAR: a tight `Ns` on a smooth sphere gives one clean dot, and
# perturbing the normal scatters it into the surface the way worn metal does.
map_bump chrome-normal.png

# `vent` is the per-texel opacity case: a solid steel diffuse punched
# through by `map_d`, so one material cuts to the shape of the holes rather
# than at a single threshold across the whole surface.
#
# It also carries all three maps at once, on the flattest geometry here. A
# normal map on the sphere only modulates a curve that was already shading
# itself; on a flat plate it is the ONLY variation there is, which is what
# makes the punched rims and the stamped border read at all. The map is baked
# from `vent-mask.png` itself, so the bevels land exactly on the cut edges.
newmtl vent
Kd 1.0 1.0 1.0
Ks 0.4 0.45 0.5
Ns 40
map_Kd crate-metal.png
map_d vent-mask.png
map_bump vent-normal.png
